{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TRYkaBAjuVSufkcVAqMApw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and propose tasks for release 05.0.\n\nRelease 05.0 contains: echo, tac, nl, fold\n\nLooking at the code_status, echo and tac already have commits on the current branch:\n- \"372e1c9 Task 347: cmd/tac: implement tac utility (prd-tac R1-R4)\"\n- \"2d0350f Task 346: cmd/echo: implement echo utility (prd-echo R1-R4)\"\n\nSo echo and tac are already implemented. That leaves nl and fold.\n\nI need to propose at most 1 task. Let me focus on nl (comes before fold alphabetically, and both are in the same release).\n\n\nI'll need to reference the PRD file for nl in the required_reading for the stitch agent, since the actual PRD content isn't included in the context. The use case is rel05.0-uc003-nl, and I'm constrained to proposing a single task based on the PRD requirements.\n\nI'm noticing a tension between the principle to split implementation and tests into separate tasks versus the constraint of proposing only one task. Looking at recent commit messages like \"Task 347: cmd/tac: implement tac utility (prd-tac R1-R4)\", it seems previous tasks have combined both implementation and tests together. I'll follow that pattern and propose the nl implementation task that covers both the command implementation and its differential tests. The PRD naming pattern appears to be `prd-{utility}` based on the commit messages like \"prd-tac R1-R4\", so I'll reference `prd-nl R1-R4` for the nl utility task. I don't have the actual PRD content, but the stitch agent will pull it via required_reading, so I can construct the task using generic requirement references similar to how echo and tac were structured.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27546,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in